{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1537764-some-thoughts-about-new-lineages-families-brainstorming","id":1537764,"title":"Some Thoughts About new Lineages/Families [Brainstorming]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Curseborne"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-06-21T11:34:26-07:00","id":1537764,"title":"Some Thoughts About new Lineages/Families [Brainstorming]","content":"So with information about Curseborne finally becoming more available, I started to brainstorm both about current Lineages and Families and which ones one might be able to homebrew, especially as a discussion about Hunters as a Lineage has already started in the forums. Obviously, feel free to comment add your own ideas and such!<br /> <br /> The ones which, I think, are the easiest to homebrew to a degree are the <b>Primals</b>, where we can frame their state as <i>the Curse is a Adversary</i>- considering that they have it manifested as a different sentience of some sort which threatens those around them. Each of the current Families revolve around a certain animal- though some are very specific, like the Get of Lyka being werewolves, while others are very wide, like the Spawn of Vodnik covering all kinds of aquatic animals. That gives the question about whether something like the <b>Kitsune</b> would make sense as its own family or not, or if all of their themes will be covered by the Get, for example, and the same is also true in regards to, let's say the <b>Grimm</b> or any other &quot;hellhound by another name&quot;. I do think that <b>Swarm-shifters</b> are a open territory, which would help to cover a wide territory of small, crawling vermin, though the idea of <b>Centipede-shifters</b> or <b>Scorpion-shifters</b> literally send shivers through my spine. I also think that <b>Minotaurs</b> make a lot of sense as a Primal family, being mostly &quot;free game&quot; as nothing seems to cover those guys, and potential some <b>Horned Ones</b> for wild elk/deer folk, which might be separated from <b>Satyrs</b> from demonic-related goat/ram/sheep folk. <b>Wereboars</b> or <b>Elephant folk</b> might also be interesting to explore, and <b>Centaurs</b> by any other name might also be interesting. While the Raptors represent reptile/avian combo, it sounds like they mostly go into dinosaur/dragon themes, which might open the door to a snakes (who are culturally differentiated), iving something like the <b>Nagah</b>. While the Spawn of Vodnik already cover aquatic animals, I feel that something that focus specifically on marine mammals, like dolphins and seals, in the form of, let's say, the <b>Selkie</b> could also be a cool idea- or even going specifically with &quot;pelt shifters&quot; in the form of <b>Skinthieves</b>. Humanity have been obsessed with anthropomorphic animals, after all, no wonder that there are numerous different new Families we could throw into this Lineage- but as the Hydes show, we might not even be necessarily limited to animals. Personal, I always had a huge draw to <b>Plant-shifters</b> of some sort, which might also be an interesting idea to explore.<br /> <br /> Another Lineage which has a lot of potential for exploration is probably the <b>Hungry</b>, which we could frame as <i>the Curse Must Feed</i>- with each application of their Curse being a different methodology through which they fulfill their unnatural hunger. On the top of my head, some kind of a flesh eating Family, such as <b>Ghouls</b>, could fall really well into their alley. However, as the Hungry do focus around social interactions, and it is unlikely that people would let them to eat pieces of their body, it puts them in a bit of a awkward place conceptually. We could move them to the more traditional &quot;eaters of the dead&quot; concept, which makes it even more complicated- as how do you ask for consent from the dead? This could potentially be solved by the Ghouls needing to ask for consent from the mourning family, which honestly, will make some sense. Looking on, let's say, V:tR for inspiration, we can also think about some other exotic forms of the Hungry- for example, <b>Breathdrinkers</b> make a very interesting idea, allowing to integrate some of the themes about the Strix and their like into the setting. <b>Memory-eaters</b> (based around the Mnemevores) could also have an interesting dynamic- removing those &quot;bad memories&quot; from those who desire it, serving as some sort of a role between &quot;therapists&quot; and &quot;brainwashers&quot;. The other main idea that I have in mind is something along the line of <b>Time eaters</b>, which act in a similar manner to the Everlasting- they trap entities in a stasis of some sort to feed on the moments on which they could have lived. Kinda a Weeping Angel vibes.<br /> <br /> The <b>Shades</b> feel a bit more complicated to me- while we don't have any shortage of undead horrors in culture, the thing is that this Family express <i>the Curse Haunts</i>- they are represented around a certain passion which they seek to feed upon, and which defines how they interact with the living world. Between misery, closure, wrath, excitement, fear and despair, it is hard to consider other many emotions which somehow resonate well with Death in order to create a new ghostly archetype. Perhaps the <b>Hopeful</b> might work? Kind of a dark doomsday cult among the dead which preaches for a &quot;better future&quot;- and potentially, &quot;true death&quot;? Could be something like the Black Heart in relation for being made of rogue members of other families. The <b>Merciful</b> might be another concept, where they seek to bring solace and peace to the living, though it might imply some very dangerous actions- might be too close to the Wardens, however. The <b>Whisperers</b> might be a different concept, representing self loath and which actively work to remind people their worst moments to send them into spirals of self destruction, but it might be too &quot;evil&quot;, I don't know. I do have some personal attachment to the idea of the <b>Drowned</b> as representing those who died in the sea, not sure about the emotion upon which they should feed, however as fear and despair are already take. Maybe envy? Could work.<br /> <br /> Moving to the <b>Outcasts</b>, we have <i>the Curse as a Exile</i>- the Curse isolates them from both the mortal world and the Outside, putting them in a liminal place where they are never belong. While we have in culture numerous different spiritual representations, I must say that the current Families do a good job at representing them- we have wish granting genie, deal brokering demons, avenging angels, nature spirits and fey, people who had a brief glance at God and were punished for it and spirits of occult knowledge and power. Still, maybe something like the <b>Unseelie Court</b> could work as trickster spirits who bring misfortune to those who betray the &quot;Old Laws&quot; as they define them. We can also go full Lovecraftian and have the <b>Eldritch</b> as a group of Outcast who claim to have their origin in the &quot;stars beyond&quot;, and which speak of foreign gods and twist the rules of physics around them, being much more &quot;liminal&quot; even than their other kin. A lot of times, however, spiritual revelations are related to prophecy, and the <b>Revelation</b> might be some ancient cult deeply embedded into human society, which follows some old &quot;instruction manual&quot; and use prophets to advance their agenda. I also try to think if there is a potential for <b>Titanspawn</b> of some kind- a group of Outcasts who claims connection to the oldest, foulest of Outsiders, kinda &quot;the mistakes that God/Evolution has made and which refused to die&quot;.<br /> <br /> Finally, we have the <b>Sorcerers</b>, who in a pretty self explanatory way represent <i>the Curse as a Sacrifice</i>. The question, then, is what could be sacrificed that isn't covered by the current families. Maybe the <b>Familiars</b> sacrifice their own freedom- vowing oaths of servitudes to other members of their Family or even other Sorcerers/Accursed in exchange for power. We could also have the <b>Deviants</b>, who sacrifice their own &quot;moral integrity&quot; by acting against the social taboos of society or their own code of conduct, depending on interpretation. Of course, <b>Blood Mages</b> feel very appropriate in here- though in order to differentiate themselves from the Reeves they might be less focused around killing and more about creating suffering- they sacrifice the integrity of their own forms, bleeding in the name of power (would also stop them from killing people in murder sprees and help to keep them different from House Bathory). There might also be an <b>Economist</b> family- which sacrifice the value of objects, people and ideas in order to draw power from it, and which have gained a lot of power from the current capitalistic system. Finally, there could also be the <b>Pure</b>, who take upon themselves numerous taboos in relation for distancing themselves from anything considered &quot;foul&quot; or &quot;tainted&quot; in order to maintain their power- touching the dead, eating meat, speaking evil, seeing evil, etc.<br /> <br /> That's just some initial brainstorming about Families for existing Lineages. Probably will take some time before I'll add some ideas for new Lineages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>"