otentially, &quot;true death&quot;? Could be something like the Black Heart in relation for being made of rogue members of other families. The <b>Merciful</b> might be another concept, where they seek to bring solace and peace to the living, though it might imply some very dangerous actions- might be too close to the Wardens, however. The <b>Whisperers</b> might be a different concept, representing self loath and which actively work to remind people their worst moments to send them into spirals of self destruction, but it might be too &quot;evil&quot;, I don't know. I do have some personal attachment to the idea of the <b>Drowned</b> as representing those who died in the sea, not sure about the emotion upon which they should feed, however as fear and despair are already take. Maybe envy? Could work.<br /> <br /> Moving to the <b>Outcasts</b>, we have <i>the Curse as a Exile</i>- the Curse isolates them from both the mortal world and the Outside, putting them in a liminal place where they are never belong. While we have in culture numerous different spiritual representations, I must say that the current Families do a good job at representing them- we have wish granting genie, deal brokering demons, avenging angels, nature spirits and fey, people who had a brief glance at God and were punished for it and spirits of occult knowledge and power. Still, maybe something like the <b>Unseelie Court</b> could work as trickster spirits who bring misfortune to those who betray the &quot;Old Laws&quot; as they define them. We can also go full Lovecraftian and have the <b>Eldritch</b> as a group of Outcast who claim to have their origin in the &quot;stars beyond&quot;, and which speak of foreign gods and twist the rules of physics around them, being much more &quot;liminal&quot; even than their other kin. A lot of times, however, spiritual revelations are related to prophecy, and the <b>Revelation</b> might be some ancient cult deeply embedded into human society, which follows some old &quot;instruction manual&quot; and use prophets to advance their agenda. I also try to think if there is a potential for <b>Titanspawn</b> of some kind- a group of Outcasts who claims connection to the oldest, foulest of Outsiders, kinda &quot;the mistakes that God/Evolution has made and which refused to die&quot;.<br /> <br /> Finally, we have the <b>Sorcerers</b>, who in a pretty self explanatory way represent <i>the Curse as a Sacrifice</i>. The question, then, is what could be sacrificed that isn't covered by the current families. Maybe the <b>Familiars</b> sacrifice their own freedom- vowing oaths of servitudes to other members of their Family or even other Sorcerers/Accursed in exchange for power. We could also have the <b>Deviants</b>, who sacrifice their own &quot;moral integrity&quot; by acting against the social taboos of society or their own code of conduct, depending on interpretation. Of course, <b>Blood Mages</b> feel very appropriate in here- though in order to differentiate themselves from the Reeves they might be less focused around killing and more about creating suffering- they sacrifice the integrity of their own forms, bleeding in the name of power (would also stop them from killing people in murder sprees and help to keep them different from House Bathory). There might also be an <b>Economist</b> family- which sacrifice the value of objects, people and ideas in order to draw power from it, and which have gained a lot of power from the current capitalistic system. Finally, there could also be the <b>Pure</b>, who take upon themselves numerous taboos in relation for distancing themselves from anything considered &quot;foul&quot; or &quot;tainted&quot; in order to maintain their power- touching the dead, eating meat, speaking evil, seeing evil, etc.<br /> <br /> That's just some initial brainstorming about Families for existing Lineages. Probably will take some time before I'll add some ideas for new Lineages.</div> </div> </div> </div>What about a <i>The Curse Overflows</i> Lineage? <b>Psions </b>practice increasing numbers of mnemonics just to function, to separate themselves from the world. It's not enough and eventually they start to spill over and twist the World with their power. <b>Frankenstines</b>or <b>Pinochios</b> are gifted with a false, manufactured soul, and the World recoils in horror from the <i>uncanny wrongness </i>of their passing. The <b>Nobles</b> are axiomatically pretty, perfect and popular, like a Disney Princess brought into real life with all attendant tropes. Unlike the Fairest of Changeling, these are not leaders who rose to the challenge. Rather, the Nobles are hollowed out by their perfection. In any case, the World is intoxicated and warped by their passage until it can not function, or it harms others because the World was so busy being compelled to fawn over you that it couldn't afford to pay attention to others. The <b>Charmed</b> are lucky, like <i>really really really lucky</i>, and the World gets caught in their adventures, weather the World wants to or not. Because while Cursed is lucky, the World in not and the collateral damage the Charmed leave in their passing is spectacular. The <b>Mused</b> are blessed with a powerful creativity, but this creativity quickly becomes an unstoppable obligation to create, until the World become full of their Creations and is being twisted into a fantasy world. The <b>Zed-canthropes</b> (A hybrid of &quot;Z&quot;ombie and Lycanthrope) are manifestations of the Curse as a Plague that can be spread from person to person, with symptoms real and occult.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1411-wingnut2292","name":"wingnut2292","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1411_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Po